  {
    "@id": "https://evergreenculturalcentre.ca/events/collaborative-clay-workshop/event_35627_0",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Event"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://evergreenculturalcentre.ca/events/collaborative-clay-workshop/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://evergreenculturalcentre.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/IMG_3212-Photog.-Quinton-Qiu-scaled.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/location": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g11029020"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://evergreenculturalcentre.ca/events/collaborative-clay-workshop/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Collaborative Clay Workshop with Xinwei Che"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "<p><span style='color: #000000; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;'><b><span style='font-size: 18.6667px;'>COLLABORATIVE CLAY WORKSHOP WITH XINWEI CHE<br /> </span><i style='font-size: 12pt;'>KAREN ZALAMEA: EVERY SURFACE IS A SHRINE </i></b></span><b></b></p> <p class='elementToProof'><span style='font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt; color: #000000;'><strong>Xinwei Che’s</strong> installations and performances embrace the liveliness of clay, making visible deep geological time by allowing change &#8211; cracking, staining, sagging, blooming. In this workshop, Xinwei invites participants to make clay forms collaboratively as a means of communication and exploration. Sensing the thickness of clay between your fingertips and another’s, an&nbsp;often solitary&nbsp;act becomes a collective exercise in close listening, negotiation, and support. &nbsp;</span></p> <p class='elementToProof'><span style='font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt; color: #000000;'>No experience in ceramics is needed – just curiosity and a willingness to work in groups! Please wear comfortable clothing that you&nbsp;don’t&nbsp;mind getting clay on.</span> &nbsp;</p> <p><strong><span style='font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;'><span style='font-size: 12pt; color: #000000;'>ABOUT XINWEI CHE<br /> </span></span></strong><span style='font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;'><span style='font-size: 12pt; color: #000000;'>Xinwei Che’s works evolve through different material states, embracing change, inviting viewers to contemplate uncertainty in a world that is on the brink of both crisis and transformation.&nbsp;She holds a BFA from Rhode&nbsp;Island School of Design (2015) and an MFA from Emily Carr University of Art &amp; Design (2022). She was&nbsp;awarded the National Arts Council of Singapore Scholarship for her BFA, and the Young&nbsp;Talent&nbsp;Programme&nbsp;Prize in 2017. She has&nbsp;exhibited&nbsp;and attended artist residencies internationally,&nbsp;including the Vermont Studio Centre, Taipei Artist Village, National Art Gallery of Kuala Lumpur, Esplanade Singapore, Richmond Art&nbsp;Gallery&nbsp;and Gordon Smith Gallery. She was recently awarded the Gwen&nbsp;Strojich&nbsp;Fellowship at&nbsp;Medalta&nbsp;ceramic center and is currently a sessional faculty at Emily Carr University. &nbsp;</span></span><strong><span style='font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;'><span style='font-size: 12pt; color: #000000;'><br /> </span></span></strong></p> <p><strong><span style='font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;'><span style='font-size: 12pt; color: #000000;'>ABOUT THE EXHIBITION&nbsp;<br /> </span></span></strong><span style='font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt; color: #000000;'>The Art Gallery at Evergreen is pleased to present&nbsp;<em>Every Surface&nbsp;Is a Shrine</em>,&nbsp;which brings&nbsp;together a&nbsp;selection&nbsp;of photo-based&nbsp;works by Karen Zalamea that traces&nbsp;the shifting boundaries between material,&nbsp;memory&nbsp;and place.&nbsp;Rooted in a critical exploration of photography’s expanded possibilities, Zalamea’s practice reimagines the medium not only as an image-making tool but as&nbsp;a methodology&nbsp;of care,&nbsp;labour&nbsp;and cultural inquiry.&nbsp;</span></p> <p>This exhibition is part of the 2026 Capture Photography Festival Selected Exhibition&nbsp;Program.</p> <div class='et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_2 et_pb_text_align_center et_pb_bg_layout_light'> <div class='et_pb_text_inner'>&nbsp;</div> </div> "
      }
    ],
